/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Activity coefficients are not independent.
   - The two-suffix Margules model assumes GE/RT = A x₁x₂.
   - GE is excess Gibbs energy (J mol⁻¹); R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; T is temperature (K); A is a dimensionless fitted constant; x₁, x₂ are mole fractions.
   - Real data are often lopsided, so many syllabi use the two-parameter (three-suffix) Margules form GE/(RT x₁x₂) = A₂₁x₁ + A₁₂x₂.
   - van Laar uses two constants, A₁₂ and A₂₁, so it can fit asymmetric data: GE/RT = A₁₂A₂₁x₁x₂/(A₁₂x₁ + A₂₁x₂).
   - Wilson builds local compositions around each molecule: ln γ₁ = −ln(x₁ + Λ₁₂x₂) + x₂[Λ₁₂/(x₁ + Λ₁₂x₂) − Λ₂₁/(x₂ + Λ₂₁x₁)]. Λ₁₂ and Λ₂₁ are positive dimensionless parameters; ln γ₂ follows by swapping 1 and 2.
   - Fit constants to measured γ data.
   - Bubble pressure is P = x₁γ₁P₁sat + x₂γ₂P₂sat, and y₁ = x₁γ₁P₁sat/P.
*/
